{"name":"Pearl White","hex":"#f3f2ed","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pearl white","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Basic bismuth nitrate, or bismuth subchloride; used chiefly as a cosmetic.","A variety of white lead blued with indigo or Prussian blue."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pearl_white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pearl White","blocks":[{"text":"Pearl Fay White was an American stage and film actress. She began her career on the stage at age 6, and later moved on to silent films appearing in a number of popular serials."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Raoul_Paoli_-_Cin%C3%A9ma-D%C3%A9dicace-Pearl_White.jpg/330px-Raoul_Paoli_-_Cin%C3%A9ma-D%C3%A9dicace-Pearl_White.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearl_White","wikidata":"Q271575"},{"title":"Pearl White (organist)","blocks":[{"text":"Pearl White was an American silent-era theatre organist and piano player who worked in the Chicago area."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearl_White_(organist)","wikidata":"Q16008477"},{"title":"Pearl","blocks":[{"text":"A pearl is a hard, shiny object produced within the soft tissue of a living shelled mollusk. Like the shell of a mollusk, a pearl is composed of calcium carbonate in a crystalline form, which is deposited in concentric layers. More commercially valuable pearls are perfectly round and smooth, but many other shapes, known as baroque pearls, can occur. The highest quality of natural pearls have been highly valued as gemstones and objects of beauty for many centuries. In literature, pearls have become a metaphor for something rare, fine, admirable, and valuable."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Various_pearls.jpg/330px-Various_pearls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearl","wikidata":"Q43436"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/The_Iron_Claw_2.jpg/500px-The_Iron_Claw_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/The_Iron_Claw_2.jpg/1280px-The_Iron_Claw_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/The_Iron_Claw_2.jpg/500px-The_Iron_Claw_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"White in an advertisement for The Iron Claw in The Moving Picture World, 1916","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Iron_Claw_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/%27Pearl_of_the_Army%27.jpg/500px-%27Pearl_of_the_Army%27.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/%27Pearl_of_the_Army%27.jpg/500px-%27Pearl_of_the_Army%27.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pearl of the Army, 1917","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A'Pearl_of_the_Army'.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Pearl_White_Stars_o